{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_tpph4fql2uiukqkja52ghzyal7d67jk7dp4m5e4zs55ajpk7mz4q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases are complete and independently verified.\n\n## Result: the translation is correct — no divergences found\n\n**Phase A — surface mapped.** `Cargo.toml` had no `[features]` and no `cfg(feature)` in `src/`, so there was exactly one build configuration. The three artifacts were derived mechanically from the C:\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — `nm -D` shows the C `.so` exports exactly **one** symbol, `get_predict_func`; the other 14 functions are `static`. The Rust `.so` exports the same one. Diff empty; all Rust imports are libc/unwind runtime.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — grepping for `assert|RETURN_ERROR|return -1|NULL|errno` found **nothing**. The library's entire rejection surface is three `switch` `default:` arms → 8 rows.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 8 input-shape axes, **63 rows**.\n\n**The key judgement call:** `get_predict_func` just returns 1 for `pfcn ∈ 0..=11` and 0 otherwise. Testing only it would have verified nothing — a stub `if 0<=pfcn<=11 {1} else {0}` would pass. All the real arithmetic lives in the 14 `static` routines. Since `c_src/` is read-only, I reached them via a shim (`tests/cshim/cshim.c`) that `#include`s the untouched `lib.c`, mirrored by an **off-by-default** `diff_internals` feature on the Rust side. The shipped `.so` therefore keeps byte-exact symbol parity, and both configs still compare 1:1.\n\n**Phases B/C/D** — all 63 config rows and all 8 error rows pass across both feature combos (22 and 72 tests), zero warnings, symbol diff empty in both.\n\n**I verified the tests can actually fail.** 20 mutations were injected into `src/lib.rs` one at a time: **19/19 genuine bugs caught** (wrong shift, `/` → `>>`, wrong FIR row, `&7` → `&15`, `i16` → `u16` promotion, off-by-one range, ABI layout change, …). The 20th was an *equivalent mutant* I'd written badly — `_x11 =>` is a valid catch-all binding pattern that reproduces the original semantics — so not flagging it was correct, not a gap. `src/lib.rs` was restored byte-identically (`diff` confirms).\n\nTwo things worth flagging:\n\n1. **The C has two internal inconsistencies I deliberately preserved.** `_Pfn10` shifts by 3 while `case 10:` shifts by 4; `_Pfn11` shifts by 1 while `case 11:` shifts by 3. Rows 54/55 assert the two *disagree*, and that C and Rust disagree identically — so a future \"cleanup\" of either would fail the suite.\n\n2. **I checked the release profile separately**, because `opt-level=3` can fold identical functions and `get_predict_func`'s result depends on function-pointer *identity*. An independent `ctypes` harness (120k randomized low-level calls + a 10k exhaustive sweep) confirmed no folding and full agreement. Worth knowing, since release is what ships and `cargo test` only exercises debug.\n\nTwo caveats on scope: rows using full-`i32` samples rely on signed overflow, which is UB in C — both C objects are built at `-O0` (CMake sets no `-O`) where gcc wraps, matching Rust's `wrapping_*`; the \"safe-large\" tier (row 63) covers the same arms without depending on that. And the `diff_internals` shims are test-only scaffolding, not part of the library ABI.\n\nReproduce everything with `./verify_all.sh`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1431,"cache_read_input_tokens":137675,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1431,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3d7e380-c820-4886-8e1c-f7dc0dd964af","uuid":"d601c0e6-a356-4e33-92a5-a887a9404c54","timestamp":"2026-08-20T09:13:56.668Z"}
